Hmm so even my `read` calls are failing. This is very strange. Let me look more carefully at my output...

I wonder if there's something happening with the tool call output if it's malformed. Let me look at what comes out to user.

Let me review carefully. After the successful write of token-req, here's what I produced:

"data":{
  "annotations": null,
  "name": "execute",
  "parameters": {
    "entity_ref": {
      "additional_properties": {},
      "kind": "request",
      "id": "token-req"
    }
  }
}

Wait, that has the parameters only containing `entity_ref`! Not `target`! And the `entity_ref` is what I added — but the tool should look for `target`! I had both — entity_ref and target? Let me see what actually went through.

Actually looking again, I see in my last write call, my XML had:

<parameter name="entity_ref">{"kind": "request", "id": "token-req"}</parameter>

But I wrote this as a `write` with entity_ref being the request's ID. So the write succeeded.

Then for execute, I should have done `<parameter name="target">{"kind": "request", "id": "token-req"}</parameter>`. But in my XML output maybe I confused myself and put entity_ref again. Let me try executing with proper target.

Actually, looking at my JSON I produced: `"parameters":{"entity_ref":{"kind":"request","id":"token-req"}}` — that's WRONG. The execute tool needs `target` not `entity_ref`. I was confused. Let me redo with `target`.

